.TH FMAX 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fmax, fmaxf, fmaxl, fmin, fminf, fminl, fdim, fdimf, fdiml \- floating-point maximum, minimum, and positive difference
.SH LIBRARY
Math library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <math.h>
.PP
.BI "double fmax(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float fmaxf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double fmaxl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double fmin(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float fminf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double fminl(long double " x ", long double " y );
.PP
.BI "double fdim(double " x ", double " y );
.br
.BI "float fdimf(float " x ", float " y );
.br
.BI "long double fdiml(long double " x ", long double " y );
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions perform floating-point comparisons and return extreme or
difference values.
.PP
The
.BR fmax ()
function returns the maximum of
.I x
and
.IR y .
If one argument is NaN and the other is numeric, the numeric value is returned
(i.e., NaN is ignored).  If both arguments are NaN, NaN is returned.
When both arguments are non-NaN and equal in magnitude (including the case
where both are zero), the value with the positive sign is returned.
.PP
The
.BR fmin ()
function returns the minimum of
.I x
and
.IR y .
The NaN-handling and sign semantics are the same as for
.BR fmax (),
except that the negative sign is preferred when both values have equal magnitude.
.PP
The
.BR fdim ()
function returns the positive difference between
.I x
and
.IR y :
.IR max(x " \- " y ,\ 0) .
If the mathematical difference would underflow, 0 is returned.
.PP
The
.BR fmaxf ()
and
.BR fmaxl ()
variants take and return
.B float
and
.B "long double"
values respectively.  Similarly for
.BR fminf (),
.BR fminl (),
.BR fdimf (),
and
.BR fdiml ().
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fmax ()
returns the maximum of
.I x
and
.I y ,
with NaN-ignoring and sign semantics as described above.
.PP
.BR fmin ()
returns the minimum of
.I x
and
.I y ,
with NaN-ignoring and sign semantics as described above.
.PP
.BR fdim ()
returns
.IR max(x " \- " y ,\ 0) .
If
.I x
or
.I y
is NaN, NaN is returned.
.SH ERRORS
These functions do not raise floating-point exceptions (unless an input
operation itself raises an exception).  They do not set
.I errno .
.SH ATTRIBUTES
For an explanation of the terms used in this section, see
.BR attributes (7).
.TS
allbox;
lb lb lb
l l l.
Interface	Attribute	Value
T{
.BR fmax (),
.BR fmaxf (),
.BR fmaxl (),
.BR fmin (),
.BR fminf (),
.BR fminl (),
.BR fdim (),
.BR fdimf (),
.BR fdiml ()
T}	Thread safety	MT-Safe
.TE
.SH STANDARDS
These functions conform to C99 and POSIX.1-2008.
.PP
The handling of special arguments (NaN, infinity, signed zeros) follows
C99 Annex F (IEC 60559).
.PP
The C23 standard defines
.BR fmaximum (),
.BR fminimum (),
and related functions with different NaN semantics; those are described
in a separate manual page.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program demonstrates these functions:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    printf("fmax(3.5, 2.0)  = %.1f\en", fmax(3.5, 2.0));
    printf("fmin(3.5, 2.0)  = %.1f\en", fmin(3.5, 2.0));
    printf("fdim(3.5, 2.0)  = %.1f\en", fdim(3.5, 2.0));
    printf("fdim(2.0, 3.5)  = %.1f\en", fdim(2.0, 3.5));
    printf("fmax(NaN, 1.0)  = %.1f\en", fmax(NAN, 1.0));
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
fmax(3.5, 2.0)  = 3.5
fmin(3.5, 2.0)  = 2.0
fdim(3.5, 2.0)  = 1.5
fdim(2.0, 3.5)  = 0.0
fmax(NaN, 1.0)  = 1.0
.EE
.RE
